## Critical implementation rules

### Keyboard injection — never use virtual keys alone

Use `SendInput()` with `KEYEVENTF_SCANCODE`. Host must use `MapVirtualKey(vk, MAPVK_VK_TO_VSC)` to get host-layout scan code from guest's VK; do not trust guest's scan code. Never use `keybd_event` or PostMessage.

### Game window scoping — only inject when the game is foreground

Before calling InputInjector from relay message handler: `GetForegroundWindow()` → `GetWindowThreadProcessId(fg, out uint fgPid)` → if `fgPid != _gameProcessId` then return (discard input).

### UAC elevation — check early, fail gracefully

In App.cs startup: if not `IsRunningAsAdmin()`, show one-time prompt "Some games require admin rights to receive input. Restart as administrator?" → Yes: `RestartAsAdmin()`. Fail gracefully if user declines.

### ViGEm virtual controller — update on pad state receipt

In InputInjector: build XUSB_REPORT from PAD_STATE (btns, lt, rt, lx, ly, rx, ry), call `_vigEmTarget.FeedXusb360Report(report)`.

### Hook suppression — only suppress when the stick belongs to a remote player

In HookManager callback: if `nCode >= 0`, check `_sessionManager.ActivePlayerId == _localPlayerId` (host has stick) and `LLKHF_INJECTED` (key was injected). If not injected and host does not have the stick, return 1 to suppress.

### Guest disconnect — host reclaims stick

When the guest who has the stick drops (WebSocket close), host must automatically set active player back to host (or next in queue).

### Hotkey — must NOT change foreground window

Use `RegisterHotKey` only (e.g. Ctrl+Shift+→). Do not use a WPF button that steals focus; show a menu or non-activating window to pick the guest.

